Thermodynamics and Chemistry — Howard DeVoe (@th-devoe: 0, is #reference, #part-of @th-sources) { r_2YHHGY26Y8X9MS9DB3MXZHD1D1
Howard DeVoe. Thermodynamics and Chemistry, second edition, version 10, January 2020. [Author-attributed full LibreTexts edition](https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/DeVoes_Thermodynamics_and_Chemistry) · [Edition and licence information](https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/DeVoes_Thermodynamics_and_Chemistry/00%3A_Front_Matter/02%3A_InfoPage) · [Historical author download index](https://www2.chem.umd.edu/thermobook/downloads.htm).

Read the whole classical treatment: systems, first/second laws, potentials, third law, pure phases, transitions, mixtures/electrolytes, reactions, multicomponent equilibrium, phase rules and cells, including the mathematical appendices and substantial problems. It supplies rigorous chemical as well as physical foundations; shared chemistry Records connect the same concepts across subjects.

The original version-10 notice is CC BY 4.0. On 29 October 2026 the historical UMD index/PDF links returned 404; the attributed LibreTexts book and licence pages were accessible. The web edition's compilation date is not a new original edition date. Check page/component notices and the version of any complete PDF obtained through the mirror. No source file is bundled.

**Return:** [Thermodynamics and heat transfer sources](../Sources.lingua#r_4N96T70VYW3C3M91M2SXF5KEQF).

**Sixth physics-core inspection — 1 November 2026:** the attributed LibreTexts contents and InfoPage again identify Howard DeVoe/University of Maryland and CC BY 4.0. Selected actual text in [4.2](https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/DeVoes_Thermodynamics_and_Chemistry/04:_The_Second_Law/4.02:_Statements_of_the_Second_Law), [4.4](https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/DeVoes_Thermodynamics_and_Chemistry/04:_The_Second_Law/4.04:__Derivation_of_the_Mathematical_Statement_of_the_Second_Law) and [4.5](https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/DeVoes_Thermodynamics_and_Chemistry/04:_The_Second_Law/4.05:_Irreversible_Processes) was inspected for the state-function/second-law distinction, reversible reference paths, boundary temperature and irreversible comparison. The common block states its Clausius premise and derives the bounded path/balance consequences; it does not claim to reproduce the complete source foundation or nonequilibrium extensions. The web compilation date is not a new original edition; no fresh full-PDF/version audit or retry of the historical failed UMD downloads is claimed. Existing edition information, assignments and rights remain, with source media external.


